WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.090
Half my class gave the same wrong

2
00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:04.770
answer to the same question.

3
00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:07.140
That isn’t carelessness.

4
00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:11.050
It’s a misconception: a wrong idea

5
00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:13.230
that makes sense to the pupil.

6
00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.900
Find it, and you can teach straight at it.

7
00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:20.990
Today: the misconceptions every class

8
00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:23.090
has, and feedback that helps.

9
00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.500
These turn up in every class.

10
00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.960
One equals sign is the same as two.

11
00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:33.180
A variable keeps all its old values.

12
00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:37.280
range of one, five counts to five.

13
00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.480
And the computer knows what I meant.

14
00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:41.240
Three more.

15
00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:43.790
Indentation is only for looks.

16
00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:46.980
What you type at input is a number.

17
00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:50.030
And if day equals Saturday or

18
00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:51.810
Sunday checks both days.

19
00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:54.730
It doesn’t: or Sunday on its

20
00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:56.370
own is always true.

21
00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:00.450
To find misconceptions fast, ask a hinge

22
00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:03.290
question: one multiple-choice question at

23
00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:04.590
the turning point of a lesson.

24
00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:06.900
Each wrong answer is built

25
00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:07.780
from a misconception.

26
00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.240
All pupils answer at once, with

27
00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:13.660
fingers or cards, and in thirty

28
00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:15.760
seconds you know who believes what.

29
00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:19.100
Read the answers like a

30
00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:20.300
doctor reads symptoms.

31
00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:23.430
Pupils who chose A think a

32
00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:24.850
variable can’t change.

33
00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:28.470
Pupils who chose C read the line as text.

34
00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:31.780
Group them, and reteach each idea

35
00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:33.200
to the pupils who need it.

36
00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:34.720
Quick check!

37
00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:38.200
A pupil writes for i in range of

38
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:40.280
one, ten, to print one to ten,

39
00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:42.160
and gets one to nine.

40
00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:44.150
Which misconception is it?

41
00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:48.640
Off by one: range doesn’t include its end.

42
00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:50.980
Don’t just correct the line.

43
00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:53.960
Ask the pupil to predict range of

44
00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:56.340
one, three, then run it.

45
00:01:56.890 --> 00:01:59.230
Seeing two numbers instead of three

46
00:01:59.230 --> 00:02:02.530
fixes the idea, not just the program.

47
00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:05.570
Good feedback points to the

48
00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:07.630
thinking, not just the error.

49
00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:11.960
Instead of: line four is wrong, try: what

50
00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:14.040
is total after the first time round?

51
00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:17.320
Instead of: you need two equals signs,

52
00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:20.460
try: what does one equals sign do?

53
00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:24.200
On written work, give one strength,

54
00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:27.160
one question, and one next step.

55
00:02:27.770 --> 00:02:29.270
Keep it short enough to act

56
00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:30.290
on in the next lesson.

57
00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:33.640
Then give time in that lesson to act on

58
00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:35.380
it, or the feedback is wasted.

59
00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:40.280
In the app, open Logic gates and pick OR.

60
00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:42.850
Flip the switches and fill

61
00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:43.830
in the truth table.

62
00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:48.200
It shows why or Sunday on its own is

63
00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:52.220
always true: OR is on when either side is

64
00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:52.480
on.

65
00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:54.250
Your turn.

66
00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:57.140
What makes a hinge question useful?

67
00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:00.410
Each wrong answer is a misconception.

68
00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:04.070
Remember: misconceptions are wrong

69
00:03:04.070 --> 00:03:06.310
ideas that make sense to pupils.

70
00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:08.910
Hinge questions find them

71
00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:09.850
in thirty seconds.

72
00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:12.570
Feedback should ask, and

73
00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:14.090
give one next step.

74
00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:17.730
And teach at the idea, not just the line.

75
00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:20.560
Try it in class tomorrow:

76
00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:22.360
one hinge question.

77
00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:25.360
Write a short program and three

78
00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:27.340
answers, each from a misconception.

79
00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:30.670
Pupils hold up one, two or

80
00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:31.810
three fingers at once.

81
00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:34.960
Count them, and reteach the biggest group.
